menu "Board driver options"

config APP_MQTT_ENABLE
    bool "Enable MQTT integration in board drivers"
    default y
    help
        When enabled, DI/DO/LED/buzzer drivers publish and subscribe over MQTT.
        Disable for firmwares without an MQTT broker (e.g. Matter-only builds).

config APP_SNTP_ENABLE
    bool "Enable SNTP time synchronisation"
    default y
    help
        Periodically sync the system clock from an NTP server and mirror each
        sync to the battery-backed RTC.  Disable for firmwares that do not need
        accurate local time (e.g. Matter-only builds).

config APP_AUX_DI_ENABLE
    bool "Offer GPIO1, GPIO44 and GPIO43 as NON-ISOLATED inputs, PWM and analog"
    depends on !APP_MATTER_ENABLE
    default y
    help
        Lets each of the three header pins IO1, RXD and TXD (GPIO1, GPIO44,
        GPIO43) be switched, per pin, in the web UI (Device tab) or with "aux"
        in /api/config: standard function, digital input, or PWM output with
        its own frequency (5 Hz .. 1 MHz) and duty cycle; GPIO1 also as analog
        input (ADC1 channel 0, 0..3.1 V).  The change takes effect on saving;
        no reboot.  Every pin starts in its standard function.  One thing does
        change with the option on: the input table in NVS has eleven entries,
        which an eight-input firmware (older releases, Matter) cannot read
        back -- going back loses the input names (README).

        Standard function: GPIO1 is high-impedance (this firmware does not use
        it); GPIO44 and GPIO43 are UART0 RX and TX, which is where the console is.
        In any other mode the pin is taken off UART0 -- the console keeps
        running on USB-Serial/JTAG, and on UART0 it simply no longer reaches
        the pin.

        The channels are AUX1, AUX2 and AUX3, channels 8..10 of the DI array
        behind the eight isolated ones.  A channel in its standard function
        reads false and is not published.

        These three pins run straight to the ESP32-S3: no optocoupler, no
        series resistor, no reverse protection.  They need external protection
        and must never see more than 3.3 V or less than 0 V.

        GPIO43 (TXD) is U0TXD.  The console stays on UART0, so the ROM
        bootloader, the second-stage bootloader and the early application log
        drive it as a push-pull output from every reset until the pads are set
        up -- and indefinitely in UART download mode (esptool, BOOT button).
        As an input it needs a series resistor of at least 1 kOhm; as a PWM
        output its load sees that traffic at every reset.  GPIO43 is
        deliberately the LAST channel, so a board that needs only two extra
        inputs can leave it unwired.  GPIO44 (RXD) is an input at reset;
        GPIO1 is unused by this firmware.

        A PWM output is under control only while the firmware runs: switching
        a PWM pad to another mode pulls it high (input pull-up, UART idle
        level), so a load is switched off with duty 0, and a driver input
        wants an external pull-down for the time before the pads are set up.

        Inputs, PWM and the analog reading are available over the web UI, the
        REST API, MQTT (aux/...), Modbus (discrete inputs 10009-10011, holding
        registers 40003-40011, input registers 30101-30104) and the rule
        engine (input(8..10), pwm(), analog()).  Not over CAN, which packs the
        input states into a single byte.

        Not offered in a Matter build.  The Matter app exposes exactly eight
        Contact Sensor endpoints and its own web UI builds eight input rows, so
        the extra channels would be invisible there — better unavailable than
        half working.

config APP_SCRIPTING_ENABLE
    bool "Enable scripting engine callbacks"
    default n
    help
        Internal: set by the full firmware when the rule engine is present.
        Enables scripting_on_time_sync() call in sntp_sync.c.

endmenu
